Replace the three site lists with one Cookie AutoDelete-style rules table.

Three prefs each held one list of bare hostnames - never delete, keep until I
quit, reset on tab close. They are the same statement with a different answer,
and holding them apart cost three things: a site could sit in two lists saying
contradictory things, the greylist had no Settings UI at all because nobody had
written a third copy of the same 260-line element, and no list could say which
KINDS of data it meant.

One pref now holds rules: an expression, a list type, and six keep flags. The
table is the one in Cookie AutoDelete's screenshots - domain expression,
per-type Keep checkboxes, list type per row.

Expressions
-----------
A pattern written as /.../ is a regular expression, the spelling Cookie
AutoDelete uses. Anything else is a host expression matching that host and
anything under it, so example.com covers www.example.com.

Host matching is by suffix rather than by folding both sides to their
registrable domain, which is what the old lists did. eTLD+1 folding turned
mail.example.com into example.com and silently widened a rule to the whole
site; a rule naming one subdomain has to be able to mean that subdomain. The
suffix test requires a dot boundary, so example.com does not match
example.com.evil.net.

Regular expressions are matched with RE2, not std::regex, and //third_party/re2
is already a dep of //chrome/browser. A pattern that does not compile is
dropped on read: the Settings page refuses to store one, so that path only
catches a hand-edited Preferences file, and a rule that matches nothing is
better than one that throws.

Migration
---------
The three old lists are folded in once, guarded by a boolean. Keep and session
entries keep everything, reset entries keep nothing, which is exactly what
those lists meant - so an upgraded profile behaves on its first launch as it
did on its last.

What is verified and what is not
--------------------------------
The matcher's semantics were checked case by case before being written, and
site_rules.ts type-checks clean under strict with stubs standing in for the
chrome:// modules. Neither is a build: there is no C++ compiler here.
